---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/race-ethnicity-and-identity">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson stands on the foundational <strong>Society and Socialisation</strong>
    lesson, which established how societies form people and how people form societies —
    the phases of socialisation, the looking-glass self, informal control. Here those
    tools meet their most consequential application: the racial and ethnic categories
    that sort lives, chances and identities. It is also the first chapter of a sequence —
    <strong>Gender and Society</strong> asks the same questions of a second axis next.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the interactionist account of identity — socialisation, the looking-glass self, role-taking, dramaturgy — and why identity is never a finished possession;</li>
    <li>state what "social construction" means when applied to race, and why constructed categories still have measurable effects;</li>
    <li>distinguish ethnicity (claimed culture) from race (imposed category) by asking who gets to define;</li>
    <li>trace how discrimination persists after bigotry fades — inherited wealth, audit-study gaps, networks, algorithms;</li>
    <li>evaluate assimilation, multiculturalism and integration as models by their outcomes, not their slogans.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One warning to carry through the page: the evidence here is uncomfortable by design.
    Sociology's job is to make the uncomfortable measurable.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Race and ethnicity are <strong>society's systems of categorising people — with real
    consequences despite unreal biology</strong>. This page sits at the meeting of two
    questions: the identity questions the unit asks of everyone (how do we learn who we are;
    how does society control us; what shapes social identity) and the specific machinery of
    racial and ethnic categories, which are history's most consequential identity
    technologies. The sociology tracks how the categories are made, imposed, resisted and
    remade — and how inequality now reproduces through mechanisms that no longer require
    anyone to intend it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="construction that works, the key distinction, mechanisms without malice, models of integration" widget="equationStep" config={raceCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four panels are the page's argument in miniature,
    and their order matters. Panel 1 reverses the folk reading of "socially constructed" —
    construction is why the categories have teeth, not why they are imaginary. Panel 2
    supplies the working distinction (who gets to define); panel 3 shows discrimination
    running without anyone intending it; panel 4 replaces ideology with measured outcomes.
    Step through them twice: once for the claims, once for the evidence each one cites.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> race and ethnicity are society's categorising systems with
    real consequences despite unreal biology — and the page will prove both halves of that
    sentence. First, though, the identity machinery itself, because categories only bite
    where identity is built in interaction.
  </p>

  <h2>How we learn our identity: the interactionist foundation</h2>
  <p>
    Identity is learned, but not by lecture — it is assembled in interaction.
    <strong>Socialisation</strong> runs in phases: primary socialisation (the family,
    language, the first "you are a..." statements), secondary socialisation (school, peer
    groups, media, workplaces, each with their own norms and sanctions). The founding
    insight is Cooley's <strong>looking-glass self</strong>: we imagine how we appear to
    others, imagine their judgement of that appearance, and internalise the result — the
    self is a social reflection. Mead refined it into role-taking: children learn selfhood
    by practising other people's viewpoints (the "I" acting, the "me" observed), graduating
    from significant others to the <strong>generalised other</strong> — the internalised
    standpoint of the community. Goffman's dramaturgy (<em>The Presentation of Self in
    Everyday Life</em>, 1959) adds the stagecraft: identity is performed front-stage,
    edited back-stage, and audiences actively co-produce the performance. Two implications
    carry the whole page: identity is <em>dialogical</em> (granted as well as claimed —
    Mead's generalised other is society's voice), and control is therefore largely
    <strong>informal</strong> — norms enforced by glances, gossip, inclusion and exclusion,
    long before law arrives. Formal control (police, courts, registration systems) is the
    hard tip of a vast soft system.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each mechanism with what it builds" widget="match" config={racIdentityMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> socialisation is something that happens to children, and
  by adulthood identity is a settled possession you simply have. The unit's evidence says
  otherwise: secondary socialisation keeps running through workplaces, media and
  relationships; identity remains an ongoing project (affiliations joined and shed); and
  the looking-glass never closes — adults still read themselves in others' responses. The
  sociology of identity is therefore a study of processes that never finish, not of
  personalities that were finished at seven.</p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="true or false — the finished self" widget="judge" config={racSettledJudge} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> identity is assembled in interaction — granted as well as
    claimed — which means the categories society applies to you are partly constitutive
    of who you can be. Now the machinery turns to its most consequential case: the racial
    categories themselves.
  </p>

  <h2>Race as a construction that works</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the biology, stated carefully. Folk racial categories do not map onto
    discrete human groups: <em>Homo sapiens</em> is young, mobile and genetically
    shallow, and most genetic variation (around 85% in Lewontin's classic analysis) lies
    <em>within</em> continental populations rather than between them; visible traits
    (skin, hair) vary clinally with geography and select independently. Aggregated
    genetic data do carry continental-ancestry signal (enough for forensics and some
    medicine), but the categories do the sorting crudely, and no ranked biological
    taxonomy of races survives scrutiny. Yet the categories organise everything:
    census boxes, marriage law, schooling, policing, wealth. This is what
    <strong>social construction</strong> means here — not "imaginary" but
    <em>made</em>: created in law and custom, enforced by institutions, and therefore
    fully capable of real effects. The proof is in the categories' plasticity.
    Omi and Winant's <strong>racial formation</strong> theory treats race as an
    ongoing political project: <strong>racialisation</strong> — extending racial
    meaning to a group — has a documented history of switches. The Irish, Italians and
    Jews in nineteenth-century America were widely treated as not-quite-white
    (Ignatiev's <em>How the Irish Became White</em>; Brodkin's <em>How Jews Became
    White Folks</em>); a century of migration, labour politics and, decisively, the
    New Deal's housing programmes reclassified them into whiteness with material
    consequences (loans, suburbs, pensions). Categories moved; the advantages moved
    with them. The <strong>one-drop rule</strong> (hypodescent) makes the same point
    from the other end: "any known African ancestry = Black" was a legal instrument
    for allocating status and property, not a biological finding — and its contrast
    with Brazil's fluid colour continuum, where siblings can occupy different
    categories, shows one diaspora sorted by two entirely different social machines.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="estimate the variation split" widget="estimate" config={racLewontinEstimate} />
  <p>
    The working distinction the syllabus wants: <strong>ethnicity</strong> is shared
    culture, language, ancestry and self-identification — claimed, flexible, plural
    (Welsh-Scottish-Nigerian-British is available); <strong>race</strong> is the
    imposed end of the spectrum, the category society assigns you regardless of your
    claim. Stuart Hall's "new ethnicities" marked the modern settlement: minority
    identities assert both — the ethnic richness of chosen culture against the racial
    assignment that keeps arriving unchosen. Who gets to define is the whole
    difference.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag between the claimed and the assigned" widget="compare" config={racEthnicCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "socially constructed" means "not real" or "no longer
  matters". The construction is precisely why the effects are durable — they are
  encoded in property titles, school catchments, algorithms and wealth, which outlive
  the attitudes of any individual bigot. Belief in biological race is false; belief
  that race has no effects is falsified daily by the data below.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — what construction means" widget="spot-error" config={racConstructedSpot} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> race is made, not found — created in law and custom, remade
    by political projects, and distinct from ethnicity by the single question of who gets
    to define. Next: where this axis sits among the other forces shaping identity, and
    what happens where the axes cross.
  </p>

  <h2>What shapes social identity — and where the axes cross</h2>
  <p>
    Social identity is influenced by the structures this unit has been tracing:
    <strong>class</strong> (habitus and taste, per Bourdieu — identity arrives with an
    accent), <strong>race and ethnicity</strong> (assigned categories, community
    narratives, minority status), <strong>gender</strong> (the next lesson's subject),
    <strong>reference groups</strong> (the people we measure ourselves against — Merton's
    addition, explaining why the same position can feel privileged or marginal
    depending on the comparison set), and <strong>choice</strong> — identity as an
    ongoing project, built through affiliation, consumption and narration. Where the
    axes intersect, effects compound rather than add: Crenshaw's
    <strong>intersectionality</strong> (1989) began as a legal observation — Black women's
    discrimination cases fell between categories written for race or gender alone — and
    became the field's standard warning that single-axis analysis misidentifies both
    problems and remedies. Du Bois supplied the phenomenology a century earlier:
    <strong>double consciousness</strong>, the divided perspective of seeing yourself
    both through your own eyes and through a society's hostile measuring glance — which
    is why self-definition (from the Harlem Renaissance to modern identity politics) is
    not noise around the sociology but data within it: a studied refusal of assigned
    identity, with measurable consequences for solidarity and wellbeing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the two classic concepts" widget="cloze" config={racAxesCloze} />

  <h2>How discrimination persists after bigotry fades</h2>
  <p>
    The modern puzzle: expressed racial prejudice has declined sharply in surveys since
    the 1940s, yet outcome gaps persist. The answer is mechanism-level.
    <strong>Historical accumulation</strong>: wealth compounds, and wealth was
    systematically denied — redlining maps of the 1930s marked Black neighbourhoods
    "hazardous" for mortgage lending; exclusion from the post-war boom (FHA loans,
    suburbs, union jobs) built today's gap. The median white US household holds roughly
    six to eight times the wealth of the median Black household (Federal Reserve
    Survey of Consumer Finances) — an inheritance machine, not a savings habit.
    <strong>Audit studies</strong> isolate present-day discrimination: Bertrand and
    Mullainathan (2004) mailed identical CVs, varying only names; white-sounding names
    drew about 50% more callbacks. Similar designs find gaps in rental replies, loans,
    and rideshare pickups. <strong>Networks</strong> carry it too: most jobs flow
    through acquaintances (Granovetter), and if networks are segregated, opportunity
    circulates inside the segregation without any decision being made.
    <strong>Algorithms</strong> industrialise the mechanism — models trained on past
    hiring or lending reproduce past sorting, at scale, with the veneer of objectivity.
    None of this requires a single intending racist; that is the definition of
    <strong>institutional discrimination</strong> — bias in the machinery.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> discrimination exists only where a bigot does — that if
  nobody in the process intends harm, the outcomes must be neutral. The audit studies are
  the controlled refutation: identical CVs, different names, and a fifty per cent callback
  gap produced by ordinary people who would deny prejudice on every survey. Intent is the
  folk test of discrimination; the sociological test is the patterned outcome, and the
  mechanisms — networks, thresholds, inherited wealth, trained models — carry bias without
  ever consulting anyone's heart.</p>
  <p>
    The consequences are measurable in bodies as well as bank accounts. Geronimus's
    <strong>weathering</strong> hypothesis — chronic stress from living with
    discrimination accelerates biological wear (measured in telomere shortening and
    allostatic load) — helps explain why US Black infant mortality runs more than
    double the white rate and why health gaps widen, not narrow, with income:
    the Black infant of an affluent, educated mother fares worse than her white
    counterpart, which is the signature of a stress pathway, not a poverty-only story.
    Sentencing, school discipline and neighbourhood effects (residential segregation
    concentrating everything — schools, pollution, policing) complete the circuit.
  </p>

  <p>
    The sorting itself is measurable, not impressionistic. The standard instrument is
    the <strong>dissimilarity index</strong>: the share of one group that would need to
    move census tract for the two groups to be evenly distributed — 0.6 means six in
    ten would. US Black-white metropolitan indices ran around 0.7-0.8 at mid-century
    and remain near 0.5 — substantial segregation with no law requiring it, sustained
    by the compounding of housing prices, lending patterns, school catchments and
    in-group preference. Massey and Denton's <strong>hypersegregation</strong> work
    showed several US metros scoring extreme on multiple dimensions at once
    (evenness, isolation, concentration, centralisation, clustering) — the
    configuration that concentrates poverty's effects. European states measure the
    same phenomena for migrant-origin groups, usually at lower intensities — a
    difference of degree that itself argues for institutional explanation rather
    than cultural essentialism.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="place today's index on the 0–1 scale" widget="place" config={racDissimilarityPlace} />

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the mechanisms — inherited wealth, name-based callback gaps,
    segregated networks, trained algorithms — explain how gaps persist without anyone
    intending them, and the dissimilarity index shows the sorting is measurable, not
    impressionistic. The closing question is normative: what should diverse societies
    actually do about difference?
  </p>

  <h2>Assimilation, multiculturalism, integration: models and their outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    How should diverse societies manage difference? Three ideal models. <strong>Assimilation</strong>
    — adopt the host culture wholesale (the old "melting pot" expectation); Gordon's
    straight-line assimilation theory charted its stages, and the evidence shows real
    convergence over generations in language and intermarriage. Costs: identity loss
    ("symbolic ethnicity" is what survives — Gans's phrase for the weekend version),
    and the demand is asymmetric — assimilation is always demanded of the weaker party.
    <strong>Multiculturalism</strong> — public recognition and support for difference
    under shared rights; Canada adopted official multiculturalism in 1971 (first
    country to do so), followed by Australia and others; critics worry about
    parallel lives and illiberal practices inside communities. <strong>Integration</strong>
    — participate fully in economy and polity while retaining identity; most European
    policy now nominally targets it. The research finding that cuts through the
    slogans: <strong>outcomes, not labels</strong>, depend on access — employment, housing
    and education equality predict integration results far better than official
    ideology does (Pettigrew & Tropp's contact meta-analysis, from the social
    psychology lesson, supplies the mechanism: cooperation under equal status reduces
    prejudice). Vertovec's <strong>superdiversity</strong> complicates every model
    further: migration flows now produce communities so internally varied (by legal
    status, generation, religion, class) that "the immigrant community" is no longer a
    usable sociological object.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="file each claim under its model" widget="sort-buckets" config={racModelsSort} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What does "race is a social construction" actually claim — and what does it emphatically not claim?</li>
    <li>Bertrand and Mullainathan mailed identical CVs varying only the names. What did the roughly 50% callback gap demonstrate, and why does it count as institutional discrimination?</li>
    <li>Why can one population — say, the African diaspora — be sorted into different racial categories in the United States and in Brazil?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It claims the categories were created and are enforced
    by societies and institutions — which is exactly why they have durable, measurable
    effects encoded in property titles, school catchments and algorithms. It does not
    claim race is imaginary or harmless: the biology fails (most genetic variation lies
    within continental populations), but the social sorting succeeds daily. (2) Holding
    everything constant but the signal isolates discrimination operating without any
    identifiable bigot — ordinary decision-makers, patterned outcomes. That is bias in
    the machinery, the sociological definition of institutional discrimination; intent
    is the folk test, not the sociological one. (3) Because the categories are social
    instruments, not biological findings: hypodescent ("one-drop") was a legal rule for
    allocating status in the US, while Brazil's fluid colour continuum can place siblings
    in different categories — the same people sorted by different social machinery.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Identity is learned in interaction and never finished — the looking-glass self keeps reflecting.</li>
    <li>Race is a social construction that works: biologically shallow, socially decisive.</li>
    <li>Ethnicity is claimed culture; race is the imposed category — who gets to define is the whole difference.</li>
    <li>Categories have switch histories (racialisation); the advantages moved with the reclassifications.</li>
    <li>Discrimination persists through mechanisms — inherited wealth, audit-study gaps, networks, algorithms — without needing intent.</li>
    <li>Segregation is measurable (the dissimilarity index), not impressionistic.</li>
    <li>Integration outcomes track access, not official ideology; intersectionality warns that single-axis analysis misidentifies problems and remedies alike.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Gender and Society</strong> runs the same identity toolkit through a second
    axis — and where the two cross, this page's intersectionality section is the map.
    <strong>Class and Stratification</strong> deepens the wealth-and-opportunity machinery
    sketched here, and <strong>Deviance and Crime</strong> takes up the policing,
    sentencing and discipline outcomes this page flagged.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Caste versus race.</strong> Wilkerson's <em>Caste</em> (2020) argues that
    "caste" — a fixed, ranked hierarchy with purity ideology — describes the American
    racial order better than "race" (a mere category) ever did; critics reply that the
    lens flattens India's caste system and that the two hierarchies differ structurally
    (caste is endogamous by ritual, US racial order was built through chattel slavery
    and hypodescent). The value of the debate is methodological: it models careful
    comparative sociology — concepts are tools, and the test is what each reveals and
    distorts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Categories as instruments.</strong> Census categories are not mirrors; they
    are levers. Changing them changes money (funding formulas, districting, civil-rights
    enforcement) — which is why their wording is always political. The 2000 US census
    first allowed multiple racial boxes; the mixed population it revealed is now among
    the fastest-growing identity groups. DNA ancestry testing adds a new wrinkle:
    identity entrepreneurship in both directions — claims staked on percentages —
    while the tests measure ancestry, not race, another layer of construction laid
    over biology.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can algorithmic fairness be defined — group parity
    versus individual treatment are mathematically incompatible in general (the
    impossibility results of fair-ML)? Does declining prejudice continue translating
    into declining gaps, or has the system entered a "post-prejudice, persistent-gap"
    steady state that only structural interventions move? And how do white-majority
    identities themselves get racialised in response to demographic change — the
    politics of the 2010s suggest identity is never a minority possession. The field's
    wager remains Du Bois's: the problem is studyable, the mechanisms are nameable,
    and named mechanisms can be legislated against.
  </p>
